
Binary Heap: 

----------------------------------------------------------------------

A Binary Heap is a complete binary tree that follows a special property.
Markdown

----------------------------------------------------------------------


A Complete Binary Tree is a binary tree where:

- All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level.
- The last level is filled from left to right.

>   left(i) = 2i + 1
>   right(i) = 2i + 2
>   parent(i) = ⌊ i-1 / 2 ⌋

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

        1
       / \
      2   3
     / \  /
    4  5 6

Quick: All levels are full except the last, and nodes fill from left to right.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Types of Binary Heap

----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Min Heap: 

- The parent node is smaller than its children.
- The smallest element is at the root.

        10
       /  \
     20    30
    /  \
   40   50


Array: [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]

Index:   0   1   2   3   4
Value:  10  20  30  40  50

----------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Max Heap:

- The parent node is greater than its children.
- The largest element is at the root.

        50
       /  \
     40    30
    /  \
   20   10


Array: [50, 40, 30, 20, 10]

Index:   0   1   2   3   4
Value:  50  40  30  20  10

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Properties

- Complete Binary Tree.
- Min Heap  → Tree Minimum element at root. 
- Max Heap → Maximum element at root.
- Used in Priority Queue and Heap Sort.

Time Complexity

| Operation   | Complexity |
|-------------|----------|
| Insert      | O(log n) |
| Delete      | O(log n) |
| Get Min/Max | O(1)     |
| Build Heap  | O(n)     |

Quick Revision

- Min Heap: Smallest element at the top.
- Max Heap: Largest element at the top.
- Binary Heap: Complete binary tree.

